The page ties stages 01, 02 and 04 to these four chains, and links the four defense topics.
notes:
Four related kill chains on csoh.org, and the page's related defense topics.

At stage 01, the page calls the malware the same class of tooling that opened Snowflake, Vercel / Context.ai and Megalodon.

At stage 02, it calls the stolen session the same property that made the Okta HAR file tokens dangerous ten months later. At stage 04, it calls the customer's alert the same pattern as Okta, where three customers found the intrusion before the vendor.

The page draws no other connection between this chain and those four.

The defense topics it links are CI/CD security, non-human identity, zero trust and incident response. Non-human identity covers the credentials that software, rather than people, uses to authenticate.
